<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=cyrillic-script">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyrillic script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">890s</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Early Cyrillic alphabet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cyrillic script is a writing system developed during the 9th century at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> Literary School in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I the Great</a>, probably by disciples of the Byzantine brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, who had earlier created the Glagolitic script. It is derived from the Greek uncial script, augmented by ligatures and consonants from Glagolitic for sounds not found in Greek. It spread among other Slavic peoples and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, and came to dominate Glagolitic in the 12th century. As of 2019 around 250 million people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> used it as the official script of their national languages.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>These texts and the <b>Cyrillic script</b> passed to Serbia and to Kievan Rus' after its conversion in 988, and Old Church Slavonic became the liturgical and literary language of much of the Orthodox Slavic world; Cyrillic is today used for Bulgarian, Serbian, Russian, Ukrainian and many other languages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cyrillic script</b>, closer to the spoken language than the traditional one, helped raise literacy in Mongolia from 17.3% to 73.5% between 1941 and 1950; campaigns using the traditional script had raised it only from 3.0% to 17.3% between 1921 and 1940.</blockquote><a href="/language/mongolian">Mongolian · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet rule brought industrialisation, mass literacy and a switch from Arabic to Latin and then <b>Cyrillic script</b>, but also collectivisation and Stalin's purges.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-ssr">Azerbaijan SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>The statute was written in Croatian in Bosnian <b>Cyrillic script</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-poljica">Republic of Poljica</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was transformed by collectivisation, the Latin and then <b>Cyrillic scripts</b>, the hujum campaign against veiling, and purges that killed leaders like Fayzulla Khodzhayev and Akmal Ikramov.</blockquote><a href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>890s</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Preslav, First Bulgarian Empire</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid">Clement of Ohrid</a> · Naum of Preslav · <a href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> · <a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Cyril and Methodius created the Glagolitic script but not Cyrillic itself; its name denotes homage rather than authorship, and Paul Cubberley places its development by their students in the 890s.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet<span>c. 800 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=cyrillic-script">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
